  "provenance": {
    "physical_parameters": {
      "origin": "inferred",
      "note": "Separate 1980-era Ural-4320 candidate based on the naturally aspirated KamAZ-740/740.10 family. KamAZ repair/maintenance manual hosted at https://dvigatelkama.ru/wp-content/uploads/2016/09/Instruction_KAMAZ.pdf, printed p.21: V8 90 degrees, firing order 1-5-4-2-6-3-7-8, bore/stroke 120x120 mm, 10.85 litres, compression 17:1, 154 kW/210 PS at 2600 RPM, minimum idle 600 RPM, no boost. Fig.21 valve timing gives intake 13 BTDC/49 ABDC and exhaust 66 BBDC/10 ATDC; encoded relative to firing TDC as 347-589 and 114-370 degrees. Full fuel 60 mg/event is inferred from rated power and approx 242 g/kWh, not measured pump delivery. 225 mm rod length, effective valve areas, thermal coefficients, fixed 15-degree BTDC injection start and 18-degree duration are authored assumptions inherited/adapted from the accepted YaMZ model; dynamic pump advance is not modeled. 650 RPM is an authored warm-idle target; 2800 RPM is audition coverage, not the rated speed. This is not a validated pressure, torque or exact 1980 production specification model."
    },
    "presentation": {
      "origin": "artistic",
      "note": "Independent candidate derived from the accepted rounded YaMZ presentation. Changed block resonances, shorter authored bank delays, more balanced bank radiation, valve resonance and reduced cycle variation to explore a smaller higher-speed mechanical V8. Transfer values are artistic and not measured KamAZ exhaust geometry. Retains 4 ms loaded pulse attack, 1.2 ms valve contact smoothing, low-fuel exhaust balance, motored flow and fuel-independent mechanical activity. Comparison recordings: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=epQ6fEGWxsQ by KAMAZ TRANS 30 (owner walk-around, variant unconfirmed); https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WnC1l6o3EYM by Oleksandr Koliada (uploader-labelled 740, recording origin and variant unconfirmed). No reference recording, reference waveform or sampled engine layer is embedded. Crankwave gas-cycle captures and procedural pressure/flow/mechanical radiation generate all running engine audio. The optional driving package separately reuses the canonical recorded starter with the accepted heavy treatment."
    }
  }
